  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how bicarbonate moves and is transformed as it travels from land to inland waters and finally to the ocean. It presents a left-to-right pathway linking agriculture, rivers and streams, and marine settings.\n\nIn agricultural soils, bicarbonate appears in the rooting zone and deeper soil, but several processes reduce its amount before it can leave the land. The main visible sinks are plant uptake, cation retention, carbonate formation, and secondary mineral formation. Arrows into the soil suggest downward movement and retention within the subsurface.\n\nIn rivers and streams, bicarbonate is transported through surface water and groundwater. Here, some processes remove it, especially CO2 degassing and calcite precipitation, shown as conversion toward CaCO3. At the same time, microbial nitrate reduction is indicated as a source that increases bicarbonate in subsurface waters.\n\nIn the ocean, bicarbonate is shown across the water column from the continental shelf to the deep ocean. Again, degassing and calcite precipitation act as losses, and authigenic clay formation is also marked as a sink. Overall, the diagram emphasizes that bicarbonate generated on land is progressively redistributed through connected environments, with multiple chemical and biological processes either consuming or producing it along the way.",
